# Auth & Session Patterns

Feature 1 replaces the starter's username/password example with Google sign-in. Spec: `features/feature-1-user-authentication-authorization.md`.

## Identity
*   Users sign in with an OC Google account only; no local passwords, registration, or reset.
*   `POST /login` receives `{ credential }` (a Google ID token). `backend/app/authorization/googleVerifier.js` verifies it with `google-auth-library` against `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` (signature, issuer, audience, expiry) and throws `GoogleCredentialError` (→ 401) or `GoogleUnavailableError` (→ 503). Tests replace `googleVerifier.verifyIdToken`; they never contact Google.
*   Accept only `email_verified === true` with both the hosted domain (`hd`) and the email domain exactly `oc.edu` or `eagles.oc.edu`; otherwise 403 `Use your OC Google account.`
*   User fields: `googleSub` (unique, nullable), `email` (unique, lowercase), `firstName`, `lastName` (empty string when Google omits them), `role`.
*   First sign-in creates a `requestor`. A provisioned account with null `googleSub` links on first sign-in without changing names or role. A subject or email already linked elsewhere → 409 `Google account could not be linked.` Never read identity or role from the request body.

## Roles
*   `admin`, `area_admin`, `requestor`, `maintenance`.
*   `authenticate` (in `authorization/authorization.js`) verifies the Bearer JWT, loads the session with its user from the database, rejects expired, unknown, or user-less sessions with 401 `Unauthorized.`, and sets `req.user = { id, role, areaId }` (`areaId` null until Feature 2) plus `req.account` and `req.session`.
*   `requireRole(...roles)` → 403 `Forbidden.` for any other authenticated role. Route order: `[authenticate, requireRole("admin")]`.
*   The initial admin comes from `npm run seed:admin` (`ADMIN_EMAIL`, `ADMIN_FIRST_NAME`, `ADMIN_LAST_NAME`), never from a first-login rule.

## Session Model
*   Fields: `token`, `email`, `expirationDate`, `userId` (FK to users, cascade on user delete).
*   Token is a JWT signed with `AUTH_SECRET`, payload `{ userId, jti }`, `expiresIn` 24 hours; `expirationDate` is derived from the signed token's `exp`.
*   On login, delete the user's expired session rows, reuse an unexpired session whose stored token still verifies, otherwise create a new one. User and session writes run in one transaction; a unique-constraint race on a first sign-in is retried once.
*   Declare unique keys as model `indexes`, never attribute-level `unique`: `sync({ alter: true })` would add a duplicate index on every restart.
*   `POST /logout` deletes only the presented session row.
*   Never log credentials, tokens, or secrets; use `safeMessage(error)` for error text.

## Login Response Payload
Flat JSON (not wrapped):
```json
{
  "userId": 1,
  "email": "avery.reed@oc.edu",
  "firstName": "Avery",
  "lastName": "Reed",
  "role": "requestor",
  "areaId": null,
  "token": "<jwt>"
}
```
`GET /auth/me` returns the same fields without `token`.

## Frontend Auth State
*   `index.html` loads `https://accounts.google.com/gsi/client`; `Login.vue` calls `google.accounts.id.initialize({ client_id: import.meta.env.VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID, callback })` and `renderButton` (via `window.onGoogleLibraryLoad` when the script arrives late; after 5 seconds without it the screen shows `Google sign-in could not be loaded. Reload the page.`). A closed Google popup never calls back; the callback ignores responses without a credential.
*   Store the full login response in `localStorage` key `user` via `Utils.setStore("user", res.data)`; dispatch `user-logged-in`.
*   `authServices.js`: `loginWithGoogle({ credential })`, `me()`, `logoutUser()` (see `frontend-services.mdc`).
*   `router.beforeEach`: `meta.requiresAuth` without a stored token → `{ name: "login" }`.
*   `SessionGate.vue` wraps `<router-view>`: on each protected route it awaits `me()`, merges the account into the store (keeping the token), and shows `Session could not be checked.` + **Retry** on failure.
*   Axios response errors (`handleResponseError`): 401 clears the store and routes to login; a 403 on any request other than `login` dispatches `api-forbidden` and `App.vue` shows `Forbidden.` until the route changes, without signing out.
*   `MenuBar.vue` sign out: 200 or 401 → clear store, `user-logged-out`, route to login; other failures keep the session and show `Sign-out could not be completed. Try again.`
